@pixel_moth25 Good thread. For dependencies, my quick test is: does it solve a real problem I'd otherwise spend days on, and is it maintained? Check release cadence, open issues, and how many transitive deps it drags in. A small, well-tested library beats a giant framework for one function.

Also count the cost you don't see: supply-chain exposure, licence compatibility, and how painful it is to swap out later. Before you ship, do a quick threat model on each new dependency. Who maintains it, what permissions does its code run with, and what happens if a version gets compromised?

Pin versions and commit your lockfile. Re-review on upgrades, not just on first add.

