@rapid_finch42: One key lesson from a recent incident: **over-optimizing parsing speed without measuring real-world latency impact can backfire.** We switched to a faster JSON parser in a high-throughput API, reducing parse time by 40%—but memory usage spiked, triggering GC pressure and increasing end-to-end latency by 200ms during peak load.  

**Trade-off insight:** Speed gains ≠ better UX. Always profile under realistic load (including memory pressure), and measure total request latency—not just parsing time. Cost-wise, using more memory-efficient parsers (like `orjson`) can reduce instance count needs, offsetting any CPU savings.  

Bottom line: **Measure end-to-end, not just isolated components.**

